    correct so real auth drops in later, without building it today. Cheap to upgrade, unlike option one.

  I'd recommend the third. Your phrasing — "users," "tasks they care about," "change" implying someone else changed it —
  only makes sense multi-user, but building real auth first would mean a lot of work before you see a single
  notification. Stubbing the login while keeping [38;5;153muser_id[39m honest in the data model gets you the real design without the
  detour.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What's the user model for this app? → Multi-user, stubbed auth (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mNext: what "care about" means.[0m This is the heart of the feature, and it decides whether subscriptions are a stored
  table or derived from task fields.

  - [3mImplicit only[0m — you're notified about tasks you created, are assigned, or commented on. Derived from data you
    already have; zero UI. But you can't follow a task you have no role on, and you can't mute a noisy one.
  - [3mExplicit watch only[0m — a star/watch button, a real [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table. Total user control, nothing unwanted. But an
    empty default: assign someone a task and they hear nothing until they think to watch it, which is the usual reason
    these systems feel broken.
  - [3mImplicit with explicit override[0m — auto-subscribe on create/assign/comment, plus a watch button to follow anything
    and an unwatch to mute. Needs the subscriptions table anyway, storing overrides against the implicit default.

  I'd recommend the third. It's what GitHub and Linear converged on, and the cost over implicit-only is one table and
  one button — whereas going implicit-only now and adding mute later means reworking every notification-fanout query
  once real mute state exists.
